# NOTES
# Totals: 128 fields checked (32 reactors x 4 fields) — 44 CONFIRMED, 32 WRONG, 52 UNVERIFIABLE.
# 1. SWEDEN "ABB" ANACHRONISM: constructor="ABB" is wrong for 7 pre-1988 units (Forsmark 1/2/3, Oskarshamn 1/2/3, Ringhals 1) — ABB (Asea Brown Boveri) did not exist until the 1988 ASEA/Brown Boveri merger; all 7 units entered service 1972-1985. Correct value is ASEA-Atom in every case.
# 2. SWEDEN TURBINE BUG CONFIRMED BROADER THAN THE RINGHALS-3 SAMPLE: turbine_supplier="ASEA-Atom" is wrong for essentially all ASEA-Atom-built BWRs — ASEA-Atom was a reactor (NSSS) design house, never a turbine manufacturer. Directly confirmed as STAL/Stal-Laval for Forsmark 1/2 and Oskarshamn 1/3; structurally implied (but unit-specific citation not found) for Barsebäck 1/2, Forsmark 3, Oskarshamn 2, Ringhals 2/4.
# 3. RINGHALS 1 RPV — a second, independent RPV error distinct from the known Ringhals-3 bug: the vessel was fabricated by Babcock & Wilcox (Glasgow, UK) with completion subcontracted to Ishikawajima-Harima Heavy Industries (Japan), not the domestic Uddcomb value currently recorded, despite Ringhals 1 being an ASEA-Atom BWR.
# 4. RDM'S GLOBAL VESSEL COUNT CONSTRAINS RINGHALS 2/3/4: en.wikipedia.org's RDM article states the firm built exactly 1 reactor vessel for Sweden (of 22 worldwide). The dataset currently lists RDM for all three of Ringhals 2, 3, and 4 — at most one of the three can be correct. Combined with the task's confirmed Ringhals-3 correction (Uddcomb), Ringhals 4 (R3's twin, built under the same domestic-content approach) is very likely also Uddcomb, though no unit-specific source was found.
# 5. LOVIISA TURBINE: current value "Skoda" is unconfirmed; fi.wikipedia.org states Soviet Atomenergoexport supplied the turbines as part of the overall reactor package, but Atomenergoexport was a trading/export agency, not necessarily the physical manufacturer — a more specific factory name (e.g. LMZ) may exist but was not found. Treat "Atomenergoexport" as a placeholder pending a source naming the actual turbine works.
# 6. OLKILUOTO 1/2 CONFIRM THE CLASSIC BUG PATTERN CLEANLY: ASEA-Atom genuinely was both constructor and architect-engineer (so those two fields are correctly identical), but turbine_supplier (Stal-Laval) and rpv_manufacturer (Uddcomb Sweden AB) are distinct Swedish subcontractors that the heuristic incorrectly flattened to "ASEA-Atom".
# 7. OLKILUOTO 3: turbine_supplier "Alstom" appears to be a cross-plant mix-up — no source found ties Alstom to OL3 specifically; Siemens retained the turbine-island scope even after selling its Areva NP stake in 2009. Alstom's Arabelle turbine is documented for Flamanville (France), likely the source of the original confusion. rpv_manufacturer is a joint effort (Japan Steel Works forging + Mitsubishi Heavy Industries), not MHI alone.
# 8. BELGIUM CONSORTIUM NAMES ARE REAL, NOT FABRICATED: ACECOWEN (ACEC-Cockerill-Westinghouse) and FRAMACEC/FRAMACECO (Framatome-ACEC-Cockerill) are confirmed identically across English, French, and Dutch Wikipedia. ACLF (ACECOWEN-Creusot-Loire-Framatome, for Tihange 1) is confirmed only on English Wikipedia — MED confidence pending a second source.
# 9. BELGIUM TURBINE_SUPPLIER IS THE CLEAREST INSTANCE OF THE HEURISTIC BUG IN THIS BATCH: "ACEC" is identical across all 8 Belgian units and no source anywhere (EN/FR/NL Wikipedia, WNA, power-technology.com) discusses turbine supply separately from the reactor-consortium narrative. Recommend flagging all 8 for re-research rather than treating UNVERIFIABLE as passive confirmation. Belgium rpv_manufacturer is confirmed only for Doel 3 and Tihange 2 (RDM, tied to the well-known 2012/2014 vessel hydrogen-flaw scandal); the "Cockerill" and "Framatome" claims on the other 6 units are unconfirmed and may be the same consortium-name-copy artifact (Cockerill being a consortium member, not necessarily the vessel forger).
# 10. GÖSGEN ARCHITECT_ENGINEER IS A CLEAN CONFIRMED BUG: KWU was general/reactor contractor, but the actual site-engineering/AE firm was Motor-Columbus Ingenieurunternehmungen AG, a well-known Swiss nuclear AE house. Gösgen's turbine_supplier remains genuinely unverifiable despite fetching KKG's full official maintenance chronology — KWU is confirmed wrong (never named as turbine supplier anywhere) but no replacement (e.g. BBC) could be confirmed.
# 11. LEIBSTADT CONSTRUCTOR should be a joint BBC/GE consortium, not GE alone — German Wikipedia names both as building the reactor and machine house jointly (unlike Beznau/Mühleberg, where GE/Westinghouse were NSSS-only and BBC supplied turbines as a separate contract).
# 12. LUCENS BREAKS THE 4-FIELD TEMPLATE: it was a heavy-water-moderated, gas-cooled, pressure-tube reactor housed in rock caverns specifically to avoid large-pressure-vessel fabrication and licensing burden. "Sulzer" as rpv_manufacturer is a category error (no RPV exists), not a wrong-vendor error. Constructor is more accurately the NGA consortium (Sulzer + Escher Wyss + Brown Boveri industry groups); Sulzer itself formally exited Swiss atomic technology work in May 1967, before the reactor's 1968 startup.
# 13. TOOLING GAP: WebSearch reached a session-wide quota (200/200 calls) partway through the Belgium sub-task and was unavailable for the remainder of Sweden, Finland, and Switzerland sub-tasks; all four regional passes fell back to WebFetch against known/guessed URLs for the back half of their research. This is the primary driver of the high UNVERIFIABLE count (52/128) — a follow-up pass with a fresh WebSearch budget, targeting IAEA PRIS reactor-detail pages, WNA country profiles, and ORNL RPV manufacturer reports, would likely resolve a meaningful share of the current UNVERIFIABLE turbine_supplier and rpv_manufacturer entries (highest-value targets: Belgium turbine_supplier x8, Sweden rpv_manufacturer for the ASEA-Atom BWR fleet, and architect_engineer across all Westinghouse/GE/KWU-NSSS PWR and BWR units where no distinct AE was found).
